                        "url": "https://github.com/takauztovies/epic-pulse/pull/57",
                        "body": "## What\n\nA pull request from a fork reports the base repository as its own `repository`. That meant a fork PR whose body said \"Fixes #N\", or that GitHub linked as closing the issue, counted as the issue's review. On a public repository anyone can open one.\n\nBoth closing routes now require `isCrossRepository === false`, which the branch-name and mention routes already required. A PR whose origin is unknown is treated as a fork's (fails closed).\n\n## Also\n\n- The query now asks for `isCrossRepository` on `closedByPullRequestsReferences`. All GraphQL fixtures were re-recorded, and the denylist check is clean.\n- **Demo PRs #9 and #10 were reopened.** They had been closed on 2026-10-07, which left the committed epic #1 fixture stale. The live test had been re-pinned to the closed state, while the README and MANUAL still showed the open one (`rev 1 · wip 2`). The live test is now back on the documented state.\n- The query cost assertion now follows `phaseBCost(1)` (5 points, as measured) instead of a pinned 3.\n- README status table and CHANGELOG (Unreleased → Fixed) updated.\n\n## Verification\n\n- A new test takes demo #5 (ready PR #10 with \"Fixes #5\"), marks the PR as a fork, and expects `todo` and no linked PR.\n- Mutation check: removing the filter turns that test red; restoring the file (byte-identical) turns it green.\n- `pnpm lint`, `pnpm typecheck` and `pnpm test` pass (498 pass, 3 skipped).\n- `pnpm test:live` passes 501/501 against real GitHub.\n\n## Known effect\n\nAn outside contributor's PR no longer shows its issue as In review. Assign the issue or push to a branch in the repository to reflect it.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
                        "isCrossRepository": false,
                        "repository": {
                          "nameWithOwner": "takauztovies/epic-pulse"
                        }
                      }
